    <h2>Part 1: The Plan</h2>

    <p><strong>Introduction</strong><br>
    This document outlines a detailed, professional, and budget-conscious plan for a Great Gatsby–inspired gathering of 25–30 guests. Each section provides clear goals, curated options, and specific cost-saving tips without compromising on style. Follow these guidelines precisely to achieve an authentically 1920s Art Deco atmosphere while maintaining financial discipline.</p>

    <!-- SECTION: DRINKS OPTIONS -->
    <h3 id="drinks">1. Drinks Options</h3>
    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Curate a selection of classic Gatsby-era beverages—cocktails, mocktails, and bulk-pour options—that convey elegance yet remain affordable. Quantities are calibrated for 25–30 guests, assuming 2–3 drinks per person.</p>

    <h4>Signature Cocktails (Choose 2–3)</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Gin Rickey:</strong> Gin, fresh lime juice, and soda water—light and refreshing.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Purchase mid-range gin (e.g., Gordon’s, ~R200/L) and buy soda water in bulk (R50 for 5L).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>French 75:</strong> Gin, lemon juice, simple syrup, topped with sparkling wine.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Substitute champagne for an affordable prosecco or sparkling wine (~R100/bottle).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Sidecar:</strong> Brandy, triple sec, lemon juice—timeless sophistication.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Opt for Klipdrift brandy (~R150/L) and a standard triple sec (~R100).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Bee’s Knees:</strong> Gin, honey syrup, lemon juice—sweet yet invigorating.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Make honey syrup in-house (honey R50 + water).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Mint Julep:</strong> Bourbon, fresh mint, sugar, crushed ice—Southern charm.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Choose entry-level bourbon (Jim Beam ~R200/L) and buy mint in bulk (~R10 per bunch).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Mary Pickford:</strong> White rum, pineapple juice, grenadine, maraschino liqueur—vibrant island flavor.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Use Captain Morgan rum (~R150/L) and prepare homemade grenadine (sugar + pomegranate juice, ~R20).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Champagne Cocktail:</strong> Sparkling wine, sugar cube, bitters—elegant simplicity.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Purchase inexpensive sparkling wine (~R100); omit bitters if cost is prohibitive.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Old Fashioned:</strong> Whiskey, sugar, bitters, orange twist—classic refinement.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Opt for an economy whiskey (Bell’s ~R180/L), and limit bitters usage.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Highball:</strong> Whiskey and ginger ale—straightforward yet distinguished.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Use a store‐brand ginger ale (~R20/2L).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Tom Collins:</strong> Gin, lemon juice, sugar, soda water—crisp and easy to batch.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Reuse leftover gin from other cocktails, buy lemons in bulk (~R20 per bag).</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Mocktails (Choose 1–2)</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Virgin Bee’s Knees:</strong> Honey, lemon juice, ginger ale.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Ginger ale (~R20/2L), fresh lemons (~R20).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Cucumber Mint Cooler:</strong> Sliced cucumber, fresh mint, lime juice, soda water.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Cucumbers (~R10 each), mint (~R10 per bunch), soda water (~R50/5L).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Sparkling Apple Cider:</strong> Apple juice, ground cinnamon, sparkling water.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Bulk apple juice (~R50/5L), a pinch of cinnamon (~R10).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Shirley Temple:</strong> Ginger ale, grenadine, maraschino cherry.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Make homemade grenadine; skip cherries if too expensive.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Nojito:</strong> Mint, lime juice, sugar, soda water.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Reuse mint and limes leftover from cocktails.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Pomegranate Spritzer:</strong> Pomegranate juice, soda water, lime wedge.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Dilute juice with soda water (~R50/5L) to extend servings.</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Bulk Drink Options</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Punch Bowl:</strong> Vodka, lemonade, assorted fruit slices, soda water.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Use Smirnoff vodka (~R150/L) and frozen fruit (~R50).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Sangria:</strong> Red or white wine, sliced fruit, splash of brandy.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Boxed wine (~R300/5L) and seasonal fruit (~R50).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Spiked Lemonade:</strong> Vodka, fresh lemonade, mint sprigs.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Make lemonade in-house (lemons ~R20, sugar ~R20).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Rum Punch:</strong> Rum, orange juice, pineapple juice, grenadine.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Buy budget rum (~R150/L) and bulk juices (~R50/5L each).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Prosecco Punch:</strong> Sparkling wine, fruit juice, soda water.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Select inexpensive sparkling wine (~R100/bottle).</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Presentation Ideas</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Glassware:</strong> Use mason jars (set of 10 for ~R100) or thrifted mismatched glasses (~R50 total).</li>
      <li><strong>Garnishes:</strong> Lemon twists, mint sprigs, edible flowers (~R20 total).</li>
      <li><strong>Ice Cubes:</strong> Freeze small berries or edible flowers inside ice (cost negligible if sourced from garden).</li>
      <li><strong>Drink Dispensers:</strong> Rent or borrow large glass dispensers (~R100–150 each).</li>
      <li><strong>Labels:</strong> DIY chalkboard tags (pack of 10 for ~R30) to identify each beverage station.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="budget-tip"><strong>Budget Tip:</strong> Select 2 signature cocktails, 1 mocktail, and 1 bulk‐pour option. Focus on a single base spirit (e.g., gin or vodka) to minimize waste and reduce costs.</p>

    <!-- SECTION: SNACKS OPTIONS -->
    <h3 id="snacks">2. Snacks Options</h3>
    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Provide a selection of refined, “finger-food” style offerings—elegant, Gatsby-appropriate, and cost‐effective. Aim to include a balance of light bites, hearty items, and sweet treats. Quantities are tuned for 25–30 guests, assuming each person samples 4–6 items.</p>

    <h4>Elegant Finger Foods</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Deviled Eggs:</strong> Hard‐boiled eggs filled with seasoned yolk, mayonnaise, and a dusting of paprika.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: A dozen eggs ~R20; store-brand mayonnaise ~R30.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Stuffed Mushrooms:</strong> Button mushrooms filled with cream cheese, garlic, and fresh herbs.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Bulk mushrooms (~500 g for R50), fresh herbs (~R10).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Mini Quiches:</strong> Assorted fillings (spinach & cheese, bacon & onion).<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Purchase frozen quiches (~R150 for 12) or prepare DIY with puff pastry (~R30) and minimal ingredients.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Smoked Salmon Canapés:</strong> Crackers spread with cream cheese, topped with smoked salmon slices.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Economical salmon (~R150/200 g) and budget crackers (~R20 per box).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Shrimp Cocktail:</strong> Chilled shrimp served with a ketchup‐horseradish dipping sauce.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Frozen shrimp (~500 g for R100); DIY sauce (~R20).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Bruschetta:</strong> Toasted baguette slices topped with diced tomato, basil, garlic, and olive oil.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Use day‐old bread (~R10) and in‐season tomatoes (~R20).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Cucumber Bites:</strong> Cucumber rounds topped with herbed cream cheese and dill.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Cucumbers (~R10 each); dill garnish (~R10).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Cheese Straws:</strong> Puff pastry twisted with shredded cheddar.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Puff pastry (~R30) and grated cheddar (~R50).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Prosciutto-Wrapped Melon:</strong> Melon cubes wrapped in thin prosciutto slices.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Substitute with affordable ham (~R50) and melon (~R20).</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Hearty Options</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Slider Bar:</strong> Mini burgers (beef, chicken, or vegetarian) served on small buns.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Buy bulk patties (~20 for R200) and mini buns (~20 for R50).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Charcuterie Board:</strong> Assorted cheeses (cheddar, gouda), cured meats, crackers, and seasonal fruit.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Cheddar or gouda (~R50 each), budget ham (~R50), grapes (~R20).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Spinach &amp; Feta Phyllo Triangles:</strong> Crispy phyllo filled with spinach and feta cheese.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Purchase frozen (~R100 for 12) or make DIY with phyllo sheets (~R30) and feta (~R50).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Meatballs:</strong> Swedish or Italian style, served in a savory sauce.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Frozen meatballs (~R100 per kg) and basic tomato paste (~R10).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Chicken Satay:</strong> Skewered chicken strips with peanut dipping sauce.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Chicken thighs (~R50/kg), peanut sauce ingredients (~R20).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Pigs in a Blanket:</strong> Cocktail sausages wrapped in puff pastry.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Cocktail sausages (~R50) and puff pastry (~R30).</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Sweet Treats</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chocolate-Dipped Strawberries:</strong> Fresh strawberries dipped in melted chocolate.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Strawberries (~500 g for R50) and chocolate chips (~R50).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Mini Cheesecakes:</strong> Individual cheesecake cups with a graham cracker crust.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Cream cheese (~R50) and biscuits for crust (~R20).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Macarons:</strong> Delicate almond confections in assorted colors.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Purchase store‐bought (~R100 for 12) and limit to two packs.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Vintage Candy Bar:</strong> Selection of Turkish delight, fudge, lemon drops, and other nostalgic sweets served in glass jars.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Bulk candy (~R100/1 kg); borrow glass jars from friends.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Lemon Bars:</strong> Tangy baked bars dusted with powdered sugar.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Lemons (~R20) and basic pantry ingredients.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Brownie Bites:</strong> Rich chocolate squares, cut small for portion control.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Make DIY with bakers cocoa (~R30); cut into small pieces to stretch servings.</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Budget-Friendly Bulk Options</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Popcorn Station:</strong> Offer caramel, cheese, and chocolate-flavored popcorn in paper cones.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Kernels (~R30/500 g) and flavorings (~R20 each).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Pretzel Bar:</strong> Soft pretzels served with mustard, cheese sauce, and chocolate dips.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Frozen pretzels (~R100 for 20) and homemade dips (~R20 each).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Fruit Kabobs:</strong> Skewers of melon, grapes, and mixed berries.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Purchase seasonal fruit mix (~R50).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Hummus &amp; Veggie Platter:</strong> Carrot sticks, cucumber, bell peppers with homemade hummus.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Make hummus from chickpeas (~R20) and fresh vegetables (~R30).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Cheese Puffs:</strong> Light cheese puffs—either homemade or store-bought.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Make DIY with cheese (~R50) or buy frozen (~R80 for 20).</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="highlight"><strong>Presentation Tip:</strong> Use tiered stands (borrow or purchase for ~R100), gold paper plates (~R50 for 50), and chalkboard signs (~R30 for 10) to label each station. <br>
    <strong>Budget Tip:</strong> Choose 4 finger-food items, 2 hearty options, and 2 sweet treats. Focus on make-ahead dishes and bulk-prepared elements to minimize last-minute labor and stress.</p>

    <!-- SECTION: DECORATION IDEAS -->
    <h3 id="decor">3. Decoration Ideas</h3>
    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Transform the venue into a lavish 1920s Art Deco environment using primarily DIY and budget-friendly elements. The total decor budget target is R500–700. Prioritize lighting and focal-centerpieces to convey opulence.</p>

    <h4>Classic Art Deco Decor</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Color Schemes (Choose one):</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>Gold, Black &amp; White: Timeless Gatsby palette</li>
          <li>Emerald Green &amp; Gold: Bold, opulent statement</li>
          <li>Deep Purple &amp; Silver: Mysterious, speakeasy vibe</li>
          <li>Rose Gold &amp; Cream: Soft, romantic elegance</li>
          <li>Navy &amp; Gold: Regal and sophisticated atmosphere</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>DIY Centerpieces:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Feather Centerpieces:</strong> Purchase peacock feathers (R50–100) and place them in borrowed vases or spray-painted bottles (spray paint ~R20 per can). Arrange on each table to create height and drama.</li>
          <li><strong>Pearl Garlands:</strong> String faux pearls (R80 per strand) or simply use craft beads (R20) on fishing line. Drape across tables or around candleholders.</li>
          <li><strong>Art Deco Printables:</strong> Find free 1920s patterns online, print in black &amp; gold at home (ink &amp; paper ~R50). Frame them in thrift-store frames (R20–50 each) for instant sophistication.</li>
          <li><strong>Vintage Book Stacks:</strong> Stack old hardcover books (borrow or thrift) with LED candles (~R50 for 10) on top for a scholarly, Jazz Age touch.</li>
          <li><strong>Mirror Tiles:</strong> Buy small craft mirrors (R60 total) and scatter them across tables to reflect candlelight and add sparkle.</li>
          <li><strong>Balloon Arches:</strong> Use gold &amp; black balloons (~R100 for 50) to frame the entrance or photo booth. Add a few “Gatsby” letter balloons if budget allows.</li>
          <li><strong>Fringed Table Runners:</strong> Cut gold plastic tablecloth material (~R50) into fringe strips and layer over a solid black or white runner for dramatic table accents.</li>
          <li><strong>Chandelier Lookalike:</strong> Hang a lightweight hula hoop (R30) with gold-painted fairy lights (~R100) to simulate a crystal chandelier centerpiece above the dance floor or lounge area.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Lighting &amp; Ambiance</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Fairy Lights:</strong> String fairy lights (~R100–150 for multiple sets) across ceilings, around door frames, or draped behind the main table to create warm, twinkling light.</li>
      <li><strong>LED Candles:</strong> Place LED candles (~R50 for 10) on tables, shelves, and mantels to evoke candlelight without fire risks.</li>
      <li><strong>Dim Lighting:</strong> Use floor or table lamps on dimmer switches (if available) or simply place a lampshade over a bulb to soften overhead lighting and create that “speakeasy” glow.</li>
      <li><strong>Gold Accents:</strong> Spray-paint thrifted picture frames, vases, or candleholders (~R20 per can) in metallic gold to unify décor elements.</li>
      <li><strong>Lanterns:</strong> Purchase paper lanterns (~R50 for 5) and insert battery-operated LED tealights for budget-friendly, portable ambient lighting.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Alternative Theme Variations</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Speakeasy Underground:</strong> Red tablecloths (~R50 each), dimmed lighting, and a jazz/blues playlist loaded onto a speaker. Serve drinks behind a faux “bar door” curtain to simulate a hidden speakeasy entrance.</li>
      <li><strong>Hollywood Glamour:</strong> Lay out a red carpet (fabric ~R50), set up velvet ropes (rope ~R20), and add a photo backdrop (ready-made banner ~R100) with art-deco patterns or gold sequin curtains.</li>
      <li><strong>Casino Royale:</strong> Cover card tables with green felt (~R50 per meter), arrange poker chips (~R50 for 100 chips), and include a roulette-themed centerpiece (DIY with craft supplies).</li>
      <li><strong>Garden Gatsby:</strong> String fairy lights in trees or bushes (borrow extension cords), create floral garlands (fresh flowers ~R50) to drape on fences or railings, and use vintage lawn chairs if venue permits.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Budget-Saving Tips</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Thrift Stores:</strong> Seek out picture frames, vases, and candleholders (R20–50 each) for a vintage appearance at low cost.</li>
      <li><strong>Dollar Stores:</strong> Purchase gold-colored cutlery (R30 for 20 pieces), black napkins (R20 for 50), and glass jars (R10 each) for candy displays or utensil holders.</li>
      <li><strong>DIY Backdrops:</strong> Use a plain black sheet or fabric (~R50) as a backdrop, then attach gold foil streamers (~R50) in a geometric Art Deco pattern for instant visual impact.</li>
      <li><strong>Borrow:</strong> Solicit friends or family for extra vases, drink dispensers, tiered stands, or fairy lights to reduce overall expenditures.</li>
      <li><strong>Repurpose:</strong> Transform empty wine bottles into candleholders or floral vases at no additional cost.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="highlight"><strong>Presentation Tip:</strong> Concentrate décor efforts on three focal areas: the entrance (to set the tone), the food &amp; beverage station (to impress upon arrival), and the lounge/dance area (to sustain ambiance). Layer tablecloths, runners, and centerpieces for a cohesive, professional presentation.<br>
    <strong>Budget Tip:</strong> Aim to keep total décor expenses in the R500–700 range. Prioritize quality lighting and one or two standout centerpieces to create the illusion of extravagance.</p>

    <!-- SECTION: HOW TO AVOID LOOKING CHEAP -->
    <h3 id="tips">4. How to Avoid Looking “Cheap”</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Polished Presentation:</strong> Regardless of the item’s cost, serve on matching trays, stands, or decorative platters. Elevation and symmetry create perceived value.</li>
      <li><strong>Consistent Color Scheme:</strong> Adhere strictly to your chosen palette (e.g., black &amp; gold) for all linens, tableware, and décor. This consistency projects sophistication.</li>
      <li><strong>Strategic Lighting:</strong> Dim harsh overhead lights and introduce multiple low‐light sources (string lights, candles, lamps) to mask small imperfections and cultivate a warm, inviting atmosphere.</li>
      <li><strong>DIY Craftsmanship:</strong> Ensure all handmade elements (e.g., signage, centerpieces) are neatly finished—no frayed edges or crooked lines. Crisp, well‐executed DIY looks custom-made.</li>
      <li><strong>Less Is More:</strong> Resist filling every available surface. Well‐spaced décor items with negative space around them feel intentional and high-end. Overcrowding signals disorganization.</li>
      <li><strong>Attention to Detail:</strong> Simple accents like fresh garnishes, cloth napkins (borrowed or budgeted at ~R50), and a curated era‐appropriate music playlist complete the illusion of luxury.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- SECTION: FINAL BUDGET GUIDE -->
    <h3 id="budget">5. Final Budget Guide</h3>
    <p>Mix and match elements to fit your overall budget. The following estimates assume moderate thriftiness and borrowing where possible:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Drinks:</strong> R1,500–2,000 (include 2 signature cocktails, 1 mocktail, and 1 bulk‐pour option).</li>
      <li><strong>Snacks:</strong> R800–1,000 (4 finger‐food items, 2 hearty options, 2 sweet treats).</li>
      <li><strong>Decor:</strong> R500–700 (lighting, centerpieces, accents).</li>
      <li><strong>Total Estimated Cost:</strong> R2,800–3,700.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Budget Tip: Borrow décor items, prepare make‐ahead dishes, and limit beverage variety to control costs.</small>
      </li>
    </ul>
