    <h2>Part 2: Brainstorm Recommendation List</h2>
    <p>The following suggestions serve as additional, optional enhancements. Use them selectively if the core plan leaves room for experimentation or if you wish to introduce specialty items for a memorable twist.</p>

    <h3>A. Drinks Brainstorm</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pear &amp; Elderflower Fizz:</strong> Pear nectar, elderflower syrup, club soda—light, floral, and refined.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Create elderflower syrup by steeping foraged dried flowers with sugar (or purchase a budget bottle ~R60).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Espresso Martini:</strong> Vodka, fresh espresso, coffee liqueur—perfect late-evening pick-me-up.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Use instant coffee mixed with sugar and vodka (~R10 per cup of coffee) if espresso machine unavailable.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Boulevardier:</strong> Bourbon, sweet vermouth, Campari—rich, bitter, and distinctly 1920s.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Purchase economy sweet vermouth (~R100) and Campari (~R120), and serve over ice to stretch servings.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Ginger Rosemary Sparkler:</strong> Gin, homemade ginger syrup, fresh rosemary sprigs, club soda.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Make ginger syrup (sugar ~R10 + fresh ginger ~R10), and pluck rosemary from a home herb garden.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Lavender Lemonade (Mocktail):</strong> Fresh lemon juice, lavender syrup, still water—aromatic and refreshing.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Prepare lavender syrup using dried lavender (~R20) and sugar (~R10). Adjust sweetness to taste.</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>B. Snacks Brainstorm</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Truffle Popcorn:</strong> Freshly popped popcorn drizzled with a few drops of truffle oil and grated Parmesan.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Purchase a small bottle of truffle oil (~R80) and use minimally; Parmesan (~R40) adds perceived luxury.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Caprese Skewers:</strong> Cherry tomatoes, mini mozzarella balls, fresh basil leaves, finished with a balsamic glaze.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Buy mozzarella in bulk (~R100/500 g) and seasonal basil (~R10).</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Bacon-Wrapped Dates:</strong> Medjool dates wrapped in bacon for a sweet‐and‐salty contrast.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Dates (~R30/500 g), bacon (~R50). Bake until crisp and call it gourmet.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Mini Lobster Rolls:</strong> Use a blend of shredded imitation crab and lobster meat in a buttery roll—small splurge to impress.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Combine imitation crab (~R50) with a small amount of real lobster (~R100 extra) for flavor, then stretch the mixture.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Stuffed Mini Peppers:</strong> Bite-sized sweet peppers stuffed with herbed cream cheese and optional bacon bits.<br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Mini peppers (~R30/bag), cream cheese (~R50). Add bacon bits only if budget allows.</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>C. Decor Brainstorm</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>DIY Art Deco Wall Mural:</strong> Use black poster board, gold spray paint, and a printable stencil from online to create a statement mural. <br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Print stencil on cardstock (~R20) and use a gold metallic spray can (~R20) for sharp lines.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Monogrammed Napkins:</strong> Iron-on gold vinyl of the host’s initials onto black cloth napkins. <br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Purchase iron-on vinyl (~R30) and a pack of black napkins (~R50); use a home iron to press.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Champagne Tower:</strong> Stack plastic coupe glasses in a pyramid and pour bubbly at midnight for a dramatic effect. <br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Use plastic coupe glasses (~R100 for 20) to avoid breakage; fill with inexpensive sparkling wine.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Projection Mapping:</strong> Rent or borrow a small projector and project 1920s patterns or Art Deco animations onto a blank wall. <br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Borrow a projector or rent (~R200) from local AV shops; download free Art Deco loop videos from royalty-free sources.</small>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Feather-Fall Backdrop:</strong> Layer feather boas in a gradient (ombré) pattern behind the main seating or photo area. <br>
        <small class="budget-tip">Tip: Purchase feather boas (~R20 each) and hang them on a borrowed curtain rod; arrange by color intensity for an ombré effect.</small>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>D. Logistics &amp; Operations</h3>
    <h4>1. Setup Timeline</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>T – 7 days:</strong> Finalize guest list and confirm any rentals or borrowed items (furniture, lighting, extra décor).</li>
      <li><strong>T – 5 days:</strong> Purchase all non‐perishable supplies (alcohol, paper goods, décor materials).</li>
      <li><strong>T – 3 days:</strong> Construct DIY décor (printables, centerpieces, signage). Prepare homemade syrups (honey, grenadine, lavender, elderflower). Freeze specialty ice cubes (berries, edible flowers).</li>
      <li><strong>T – 1 day:</strong> Cook/bake foods that keep well (deviled eggs, quiches, brownies). Assemble label tags and finalize station layouts. Chill beverages in small batches.</li>
      <li><strong>Event Day (Day 0):</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>Arrive at venue by noon. Unload and designate zones (entrance, bar, snack table, lounge). Stage major décor focal points (balloon arch, centerpiece tables, photo area).</li>
          <li>Setup furniture, tablecloths, runners, and tiered stands. Arrange lighting (fairy lights, LED candles, lamps). Test all light sources and adjust dimmers if available.</li>
          <li>One hour before arrival: Fill drink dispensers, stock bar with garnishes and glassware. Place snack trays, finalize garnish placements, and fluff all pillows/soft seating. Cue curated Jazz Age playlist to begin once guests arrive.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h4>2. Roles &amp; Assignments</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lead Bartender:</strong> Person responsible for managing the bar station, mixing signature cocktails, restocking glassware, and ensuring garnishes are fresh. Recommended: someone with bartending experience or strong organizational skills.</li>
      <li><strong>Snack Sous-Chef:</strong> Person in charge of plating, replenishing, and maintaining snack stations (finger foods, hearty options, sweet treats). Recommended: someone proficient with kitchen tasks and time management.</li>
      <li><strong>Décor Coordinator:</strong> Person responsible for last-minute décor touches (balloon arch repairs, flower arrangements, signage placement). Recommended: a friend with craft skills or a keen eye for staging.</li>
      <li><strong>Vibe Manager:</strong> Person overseeing ambient details—monitoring the music playlist, adjusting lighting levels, and responding to guest feedback about atmosphere. Recommended: someone familiar with the event flow and able to multitask discreetly.</li>
      <li><strong>Floater:</strong> Person designated to “refill” stations—restocking charcuterie boards, topping up punch bowls, clearing used plates/glassware from tables. Recommended: someone with a friendly demeanor who can move unobtrusively.</li>
    </ul>

